1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,416
A cricket bat.

2
00:00:01,416 --> 00:00:02,383
A steel plate.

3
00:00:02,383 --> 00:00:03,283
A bangle.

4
00:00:03,084 --> 00:00:05,684
Each has one point where
its mass is centred.

5
00:00:06,084 --> 00:00:08,601
For some bodies that
point is easy to find.

6
00:00:08,601 --> 00:00:10,450
For others, it is a surprise.

7
00:00:10,901 --> 00:00:14,332
Aarav tapes a bangle onto a
light, clear plastic square.

8
00:00:14,332 --> 00:00:16,163
It balances on his fingertip.

9
00:00:16,513 --> 00:00:20,368
But it balances right at the bangle's
centre, where there is no metal at

10
00:00:20,368 --> 00:00:21,268
all.

11
00:00:20,801 --> 00:00:21,701
How?

12
00:00:21,734 --> 00:00:24,420
Let's find the centre of
mass of common bodies.

13
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:26,931
Start with a uniform rod.

14
00:00:26,931 --> 00:00:29,767
Uniform means its mass is spread evenly.

15
00:00:30,117 --> 00:00:31,337
Pair up the bits.

16
00:00:31,337 --> 00:00:34,870
A bit near the left end matches
a bit near the right end.

17
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:37,202
Every pair averages out at the middle.

18
00:00:37,202 --> 00:00:39,496
So the rod's centre of
mass is at its middle.

19
00:00:39,896 --> 00:00:44,606
The same pairing works for a ring,
a disc, a sphere and a rectangle.

20
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:49,356
Uniform and symmetric means the centre
of mass is at the geometric centre.

21
00:00:49,956 --> 00:00:52,330
A uniform triangle plate is different.

22
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:55,545
Its centre of mass is where
the three medians meet.

23
00:00:56,145 --> 00:00:58,399
Now let's build Aarav's
bangle, bit by bit.

24
00:00:58,399 --> 00:00:59,752
Radius twenty centimetres.

25
00:01:00,102 --> 00:01:02,149
First, a small arc of metal.

26
00:01:02,149 --> 00:01:05,050
Its centre of mass sits
just inside the arc.

27
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:06,500
Grow the arc.

28
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,905
As it bends round, the centre of
mass moves in, toward the middle.

29
00:01:11,305 --> 00:01:14,588
At a half ring, it is two
R over pi from the middle.

30
00:01:14,588 --> 00:01:17,650
About twelve point seven
centimetres, in empty space.

31
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:19,221
Slow-mo replay.

32
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:21,022
Watch the last gap close.

33
00:01:21,372 --> 00:01:24,404
Every new bit now has a
partner on the far side.

34
00:01:24,804 --> 00:01:27,732
At the full ring, the
distance drops to zero.

35
00:01:27,732 --> 00:01:31,526
The centre of mass is the ring's
centre, with no metal there.

36
00:01:32,126 --> 00:01:33,026
Quick check.

37
00:01:33,026 --> 00:01:34,511
Cut the ring in half.

38
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:38,632
Is the half ring's centre of mass
on the metal, or in empty space?

39
00:01:38,632 --> 00:01:39,820
Pause and decide.

40
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:43,904
In empty space!

41
00:01:43,904 --> 00:01:47,843
It sits twelve point seven centimetres
from the middle, inside the curve.

42
00:01:48,443 --> 00:01:50,713
Now the rules, one step at a time.

43
00:01:51,063 --> 00:01:52,002
Step one.

44
00:01:52,002 --> 00:01:56,881
A bit of mass m at plus x pairs
with an equal bit at minus x.

45
00:01:56,881 --> 00:02:00,018
m x, plus m times minus x, is zero.

46
00:02:00,768 --> 00:02:01,668
Step two.

47
00:02:01,611 --> 00:02:05,105
So a uniform, symmetric
body has X at its centre.

48
00:02:05,105 --> 00:02:08,362
A rod of length L has
X equal to L over two.

49
00:02:09,112 --> 00:02:10,093
Step three.

50
00:02:10,093 --> 00:02:14,773
For a body made of parts, put each
part's mass at its own centre.

51
00:02:14,773 --> 00:02:19,485
X equals M one X one plus M two
X two, over M one plus M two.

52
00:02:20,235 --> 00:02:21,135
Step four.

53
00:02:21,125 --> 00:02:24,706
For a uniform plate, mass
is in proportion to area.

54
00:02:24,706 --> 00:02:27,397
So we can use areas instead of masses.

55
00:02:28,147 --> 00:02:29,102
Remember this.

56
00:02:29,102 --> 00:02:31,684
The centre of mass can sit in empty space.

57
00:02:31,684 --> 00:02:33,989
It depends on how the mass is spread.

58
00:02:34,689 --> 00:02:35,712
Side by side.

59
00:02:35,712 --> 00:02:38,307
A ring: all its mass sits on the rim.

60
00:02:38,707 --> 00:02:41,162
A disc: its mass is spread all over.

61
00:02:41,162 --> 00:02:43,887
A different spread,
but both are symmetric.

62
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:45,505
Now you try.

63
00:02:45,505 --> 00:02:48,559
A ring and a disc, same mass, same radius.

64
00:02:48,559 --> 00:02:51,159
Whose centre of mass is at the centre?

65
00:02:51,159 --> 00:02:52,424
Pause and decide.

66
00:02:55,624 --> 00:02:56,524
Both!

67
00:02:56,134 --> 00:02:58,003
Each is uniform and symmetric.

68
00:02:58,003 --> 00:03:00,299
For the disc, that point has metal.

69
00:03:00,299 --> 00:03:02,131
For the ring, it is empty.

70
00:03:02,781 --> 00:03:04,471
Now, one worked example.

71
00:03:04,821 --> 00:03:07,433
An L-shaped plate is
made of two rectangles.

72
00:03:07,433 --> 00:03:10,181
One is forty by ten
centimetres, lying flat.

73
00:03:10,181 --> 00:03:13,500
The other is ten by thirty,
standing on its left end.

74
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:15,087
Find the centre of mass.

75
00:03:15,587 --> 00:03:18,464
Put the origin at the bottom left corner.

76
00:03:18,464 --> 00:03:23,307
The flat part has area four hundred,
with its centre at twenty, five.

77
00:03:24,057 --> 00:03:28,860
The standing part has area three
hundred, with its centre at five, twenty

78
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:29,760
five.

79
00:03:30,185 --> 00:03:34,626
X: four hundred times twenty, plus
three hundred times five, over seven

80
00:03:34,626 --> 00:03:35,526
hundred.

81
00:03:35,308 --> 00:03:37,615
About thirteen point six centimetres.

82
00:03:37,615 --> 00:03:39,153
Y comes out the same.

83
00:03:39,903 --> 00:03:43,752
So the centre is at thirteen
point six, thirteen point six.

84
00:03:43,752 --> 00:03:47,055
That point is off the
plate, in the empty corner.

85
00:03:47,705 --> 00:03:49,167
Now, the exam trap.

86
00:03:49,517 --> 00:03:53,191
When a piece is cut out of a plate,
many students add the piece's mass.

87
00:03:53,591 --> 00:03:55,296
But a hole removes mass.

88
00:03:55,296 --> 00:03:58,552
Treat the hole as negative
mass, and subtract it.

89
00:03:59,202 --> 00:04:00,425
Where is this asked?

90
00:04:00,425 --> 00:04:03,979
In composite bodies, and in plates
or discs with a part cut out.

91
00:04:04,579 --> 00:04:05,616
Exam level now.

92
00:04:05,616 --> 00:04:08,684
A uniform square plate has
sides of forty centimetres.

93
00:04:08,684 --> 00:04:12,078
A square of side twenty
centimetres is cut from one corner.

94
00:04:12,078 --> 00:04:15,251
How far does the centre of
mass move, and which way?

95
00:04:15,251 --> 00:04:16,333
Pause and solve.

96
00:04:21,733 --> 00:04:22,867
The answer is A.

97
00:04:22,867 --> 00:04:26,387
About four point seven centimetres,
away from the cut corner.

98
00:04:26,887 --> 00:04:29,633
Full plate: mass four m, at the centre.

99
00:04:29,633 --> 00:04:33,563
The cut piece: mass m, ten
centimetres along x and along y.

100
00:04:34,063 --> 00:04:35,105
Subtract it.

101
00:04:35,105 --> 00:04:38,264
X equals minus m times ten, over three m.

102
00:04:38,264 --> 00:04:39,918
Minus ten over three.

103
00:04:39,918 --> 00:04:41,197
Y is the same.

104
00:04:41,697 --> 00:04:45,813
Distance: root two times ten over
three, about four point seven.

105
00:04:45,813 --> 00:04:48,421
The minus sign points away from the cut.

106
00:04:49,021 --> 00:04:50,561
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:50,561 --> 00:04:52,144
Screenshot this card.

108
00:04:55,344 --> 00:04:57,282
Can you answer these without help?

109
00:04:57,682 --> 00:04:58,582
One.

110
00:04:58,124 --> 00:05:01,124
Why is a uniform rod's
centre of mass at its middle?

111
00:05:02,024 --> 00:05:02,924
Two.

112
00:05:02,411 --> 00:05:05,395
How can a ring's centre of mass
be where there is no metal?

113
00:05:06,295 --> 00:05:07,195
Three.

114
00:05:06,712 --> 00:05:08,834
How do you handle a
hole cut out of a plate?

115
00:05:09,734 --> 00:05:12,891
Next time: a festival
rocket bursts in the sky.

116
00:05:12,891 --> 00:05:16,847
The pieces fly everywhere, yet
one point keeps the old curve.

117
00:05:16,847 --> 00:05:18,854
Motion of the centre of mass.
